Real-Time Social Media/News Feed Analyzer is very modern and emphasizes both text processing and advanced MongoDB aggregation techniques like $addFields and $bucket.Here is a focused plan detailing the technologies, data model, and specific analysis steps for your group.📰 Project Plan: Social Media Feed Analyzer1. 🛠️ Technology Stack (Recommended)ComponentTechnologyRationaleBackend/Data ProcessingPythonPython has the best libraries for text analysis (like NLTK or TextBlob for sentiment) and connecting to MongoDB (PyMongo).Web FrameworkFlask or DjangoSimple frameworks to host your application logic and serve the visualization page.DatabaseMongoDBThe core data store, leveraging its flexible schema for unstructured text data.VisualizationChart.js or D3.jsJavaScript libraries for creating interactive charts from the aggregated JSON data.2. 🗄️ Data Model & IngestionSince you are simulating a "real-time" feed, you'll work with a static dataset containing several hundred thousand to a few million documents.Data Source: Use an available dataset of social media posts (e.g., archived Twitter/X data, Reddit submissions, or news headlines) that includes a timestamp, text, and some measure of engagement (likes, comments, upvotes).Collection Schema:FieldTypeDescription_idObjectIdMongoDB's primary key.timestampDateThe time the post was made. Crucial for trend analysis.contentStringThe main text of the post/article.author_idStringIdentifier for the user/author.engagement_scoreIntegerTotal reactions (likes + shares + comments).tagsArray of StringsHashtags or categorized topics.Ingestion Step: Write a Python script using PyMongo to read your raw data (e.g., CSV or JSON file) and insert it into a MongoDB collection.3. 🧠 Core Data Processing & AnalysisThis is the most critical part, where you use MongoDB's Aggregation Pipeline to analyze the "big data." You will perform the following analysis steps in your application code before displaying them.A. Sentiment Trend Analysis (Temporal Grouping)Goal: Calculate the average sentiment of all posts for each day of the week or hour of the day to identify patterns.Preprocessing: First, your Python script must calculate and store a sentiment score (a numerical value, e.g., $-1$ to $+1$) for each post using a library like TextBlob. Store this in a new field called sentiment_score.MongoDB Pipeline:$project: Extract the day-of-week/hour from the timestamp.$group: Group the documents by the extracted day/hour.$avg: Calculate the average of the sentiment_score for each group.$sort: Order the results.$$\begin{aligned}
[
&\text{\$project}: \{ \_id: 0, \text{dayOfWeek}: \{ \text{\$dayOfWeek}: \text{\$timestamp} \}, \text{score}: \text{\$sentiment\_score} \}, \\
